##### Main result (informal; refer to Section 4).

Our contribution is a sharp hypothesis-class characterization of when realizable strong universal Bayes-consistency is possible for general metric losses. The characterization is in terms of a single combinatorial obstruction: an *unbounded-gap* Littlestone tree.

Informally, an infinite non-decreasing (γk)(\gamma\_{k})-Littlestone tree is a full binary tree of instances whose depth-kk nodes admit two outgoing labels at metric distance at least γk\gamma\_{k}, where γk↑∞\gamma\_{k}\uparrow\infty, and such that every finite root-to-leaf labeling pattern is realizable by some h∈ℋh\in{\mathcal{H}} (Definition [4.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S4.Thmtheorem1 "Definition 4.1 (non-decreasing-𝛾_𝑘-Littlestone tree). ‣ 4 Main result: characterization of ℋ ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")). Under a mild compact-parameterization assumption (Lemma [4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S4.Thmtheorem3 "Lemma 4.3 (Bridging lemma: finite-prefix realizability implies infinite-path realizability). ‣ 4 Main result: characterization of ℋ ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")), this finite-prefix condition automatically ensures that every *infinite* path is realized by a single hypothesis. In particular, the counterexample of Section [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S3 "3 𝑅^∗<∞ is insufficient ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses") naturally satisfies this assumption, demonstrating that unlearnability remains possible even under it (see Appendix [A.4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#A1.SS4.SSS3 "A.4.3 The assumption does not rule out unlearnability ‣ A.4 On the compact-parameterization assumption ‣ Appendix A Supplementary material ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses") for details).

