The BIE condition is a natural metric analogue of the real-valued moment assumption 𝔼|Y|<∞\mathop{\mathbb{E}}|Y|<\infty. It was shown to be sufficient for Bayes consistency of 𝖬𝖾𝖽𝖭𝖾𝗍{\mathsf{MedNet}}, and it motivates the broader question of what, precisely, is required for strong Bayes-consistency when the loss is unbounded. Accordingly, Tsir Cohen & Kontorovich ([2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#bib.bib10)) posed the open problem of giving a necessary and sufficient condition on (𝒳,ρ)({\mathcal{X}},\rho), (𝒴,ℓ)({\mathcal{Y}},\ell), and μ¯{\bar{\mu}} under which 𝖬𝖾𝖽𝖭𝖾𝗍{\mathsf{MedNet}}- or some other learning algorithm - is strongly Bayes-consistent. A natural and optimistic candidate was the finiteness of the Bayes risk, R∗<∞R^{\*}<\infty. In this section we show that this condition is not sufficient: without further assumptions, no strongly universally Bayes-consistent learner can exist.

