The upper bound proceeds in four steps: (1) we encode the tree obstruction as a Gale-Stewart game and invoke the measurable-strategy machinery of Bousquet et al. ([2020](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#bib.bib3)) (Section [6.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS1 "6.1 A Gale-Stewart game ‣ 6 Upper bound: absence of an infinite tree implies strong universal consistency ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")); (2) we show that driving this game on an i.i.d. sample causes it to *stabilize* after finitely many rounds a.s., producing for each xx a set of admissible labels HK​(x)H\_{K}(x) of bounded diameter (Sections [6.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS2 "6.2 History-conditional label sets ‣ 6 Upper bound: absence of an infinite tree implies strong universal consistency ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")-[6.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS3 "6.3 Stabilization on an infinite i.i.d. sample ‣ 6 Upper bound: absence of an infinite tree implies strong universal consistency ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")); (3) we partition 𝒳{\mathcal{X}} into countably many cells, each with a common bounded label region (Section [6.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS4 "6.4 Reducing per-𝑥 bounded diameter to countably many bounded-range subproblems ‣ 6 Upper bound: absence of an infinite tree implies strong universal consistency ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")); (4) on each cell we apply the bounded-range learner 𝖬𝖾𝖽𝖭𝖾𝗍{\mathsf{MedNet}} of Tsir Cohen & Kontorovich ([2022](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#bib.bib10)) and aggregate (Sections [6.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS5 "6.5 A realizable learning rule ‣ 6 Upper bound: absence of an infinite tree implies strong universal consistency ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses")-[6.6](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS6 "6.6 Realizable strong universal Bayes-consiste