##### Standing measurability assumptions.

To avoid measure-theoretic pathologies (measurable determinacy / measurable strategies and measurable selections), we assume that both (𝒳,ρ)({\mathcal{X}},\rho) and (𝒴,ℓ)({\mathcal{Y}},\ell) are Polish spaces. In particular, (𝒴,ℓ)({\mathcal{Y}},\ell) is separable, hence admits a countable dense subset, which we will use to build a countable partition of 𝒳{\mathcal{X}}. In addition, we assume the hypothesis class ℋ{\mathcal{H}} satisfies the measurability condition of Bousquet et al. ([2020](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#bib.bib3), Definition 3.3): there exists a Polish space Θ\Theta (the *parameter space*) and a Borel-measurable *evaluation map* h:Θ×𝒳→𝒴h\colon\Theta\times{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{Y}}, (θ,x)↦h​(θ,x)(\theta,x)\mapsto h(\theta,x), such that ℋ={h​(θ,⋅):θ∈Θ}{\mathcal{H}}=\{h(\theta,\cdot):\theta\in\Theta\}. We strengthen this by requiring Θ\Theta to be compact and hh to be continuous in θ\theta (i.e., θ↦h​(θ,x)\theta\mapsto h(\theta,x) is continuous for each fixed x∈𝒳x\in{\mathcal{X}}; cf. Lemma [4.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S4.Thmtheorem3 "Lemma 4.3 (Bridging lemma: finite-prefix realizability implies infinite-path realizability). ‣ 4 Main result: characterization of ℋ ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses"), and see Appendix [A.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#A1.SS4 "A.4 On the compact-parameterization assumption ‣ Appendix A Supplementary material ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses") for a discussion of why this strengthening is needed and why it is mild), so that the game in Section [6.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#S6.SS1 "6.1 A Gale-Stewart game ‣ 6 Upper bound: absence of an infinite tree implies strong universal consistency ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses") admits a *universally measurable* winning strategy for the learner. Whenever we define set-valued maps or indices using this strategy (e.g. Hk​(⋅)H\_{k}(\cdot), jk​(⋅)j\_{k}(\cdot), and the cells