#### A.4.4 Examples of settings satisfying the assumption

* • Finite or compact label spaces. When 𝒴{\mathcal{Y}} is compact (e.g., binary classification with 𝒴={0,1}{\mathcal{Y}}=\{0,1\}, multi-class classification with finite 𝒴{\mathcal{Y}}, or bounded regression with 𝒴=[a,b]{\mathcal{Y}}=[a,b]), the function space 𝒴𝒳{\mathcal{Y}}^{\mathcal{X}} is compact in the product topology by Tychonoff’s theorem, and the classical compactness argument of Bousquet et al. ([2020](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#bib.bib3)) shows that the two tree notions coincide automatically. Our compact-parameterization assumption is not needed in this case and is strictly weaker than compactness of 𝒴{\mathcal{Y}}. * • Parametric families with bounded parameters. Neural networks with bounded weights, support vector machines with bounded kernels, and Lipschitz functions on compact domains all naturally have compact parameter spaces (e.g., Θ=[−W,W]d\Theta=[-W,W]^{d}) and continuous evaluation maps. * • The counterexample is pathological. The separating example of Section [A.4.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03823v2#A1.SS4.SSS2 "A.4.2 Why the assumption is necessary ‣ A.4 On the compact-parameterization assumption ‣ Appendix A Supplementary material ‣ Realizable Bayes-Consistency for General Metric Losses") uses Θ=ℕ0\Theta=\mathbb{N}\_{0} (discrete, non-compact) and a discontinuous evaluation map. Classes parametrized by a countably infinite discrete space with no accumulation points are unusual in practical learning settings. In contrast, the compact-parameterization assumption permits Θ\Theta to be any compact Polish space, which includes all compact subsets of ℝd\mathbb{R}^{d}, compact manifolds, and many infinite-dimensional spaces (e.g., compact subsets of function spaces in the compact-open topology).

